# Operator-identity, decay-rate and energy-balance test for the Braginskii
# electron viscous DRAG in Ohm's law (hybrid_pic_model.qdsmc_viscosity_in_ohms_law = 1),
# energy-balanced with the booked viscous heating.
#
# Same manufactured state as inputs_test_3d_ohm_solver_hyper_res_heating: a 3D
# periodic box, B_z(x) = B0 + b1 cos(k x), cold ions at rest, so the plasma
# current is the single mode J_y(x) = J0 sin(k x) and the electron fluid
# velocity is u_e = -J/(e n0). Every other Ohm's-law term is off, so the
# Faraday electric field is the viscous drag alone.
#
# ISOTROPIC arm (this file): parser model with nu_par = nu_perp = nu, no
# limiter, no free-streaming cap. Then Pi = -mu W (mu = m_e n0 nu), and for
# the divergence-free planar shear u_y(x) the drag reduces EXACTLY to a
# hyper-resistivity with eta_eff = m_e nu/(e^2 n0):
#     E_visc,y = eta_eff * kc^2 * J_y      (discrete symbol kc = sin(k dx)/dx of
#                                          the centred nodal gradient, applied twice)
# so the mode energy decays as exp(-2 gamma t), gamma = eta_eff kc^2 ky^2 / mu0
# with ky = 2 sin(k dx/2)/dx the Yee curl symbol (T1: operator identity via
# the discrete symbol; T3: decay rate). nu = 281.7 m^2/s puts eta_eff at
# 1.0e-9 Ohm m^3, the value of the eta_H fixture, so the two arms compare
# directly (they differ by the (kc/ky)^2 = cos^2(k dx/2) symbol ratio, 0.962
# here, and both converge to eta k^4/mu0).
#
# T2: with qdsmc_viscosity_heating = strain (set explicitly here; the
# default is work) the ledger books Q_nu = -Pi:grad u_e (visc_bulk) and
# reports the drag work J . E_visc (visc_work_bulk); with = work (default)
# it books the work itself. Both must close against the magnetic energy
# Faraday removes; with ions at rest the two agree to round-off because the
# drag is the exact adjoint of the centred gradient chain.
#
# COMPRESSIVE / 2D arm (diagonal legs of div Pi and a genuinely 2D
# structure): pass
#   warpx.Bz_external_grid_function(x,y,z)="0.1 + 0.005*cos(41.887902047863905*x)*cos(83.77580409572781*y)"
#   warpx.const_dt=4.0e-9
# so J_x(x,y) and J_y(x,y) are both nonzero with G_xx = -G_yy != 0;
# analysis_viscous_drag.py --aniso checks the closure (no closed-form rate).
#
# ANISOTROPIC arm: pass
#   warpx.Bx_external_grid_function(x,y,z)="0.0707106781186548"
#   warpx.By_external_grid_function(x,y,z)="0.005*sin(41.887902047863905*x)"
#   warpx.Bz_external_grid_function(x,y,z)="0.0707106781186548"
#   hybrid_pic_model.qdsmc_nu_perp(n,Te,B)="0.2817"
# so the current is J_z(x) and the field-aligned strain S = 2 b_x b_z G_xz is
# nonzero (b tilted 45 deg); analysis_viscous_drag.py --aniso then checks the
# strain/work/field closure only (no closed-form rate).

